    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d311">

                <pb facs="0435" n="415" xml:id="pg415"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">661.91/7–1850: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in India</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Henderson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">niact</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">New Delhi</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1950-07-18T13:00:00+05:30">July 18, 1950—1 p.
                            m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received July 18—8:31 a. m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>132. Bajpai SYG MEA called me noon today to say Soviet Government Moscow had
                    published texts of recent exchange of notes with GOI without securing consent of
                    latter. Bajpai added that he hoped that US government would not release either
                    message until after agreement between the two governments for simultaneously
                    release in Washington and Delhi. He referred to press report to effect US
                    planned release messages at time delivery and said he thought this report must
                    be inaccurate.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Henderson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
